import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse('{"title":"Ports are types; implementations are named for what they do","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Ports are types; implementations are named for what they do","type":"adr","date":"2026-10-29T00:00:00.000Z","status":"proposed","status_note":"Proposed — 2026-10-28. Owner: every `lib-*` repo with a Rust shell."},"headers":[],"relativePath":"handl/project/adrs/0161-ports-are-types-impls-are-named-for-what-they-do.md","filePath":"vendored/handl/project/adrs/0161-ports-are-types-impls-are-named-for-what-they-do.md"}'),a={name:`handl/project/adrs/0161-ports-are-types-impls-are-named-for-what-they-do.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="adr-0161-ports-are-types-implementations-are-named-for-what-they-do" tabindex="-1">ADR-0161: Ports are types; implementations are named for what they do <a class="header-anchor" href="#adr-0161-ports-are-types-implementations-are-named-for-what-they-do" aria-label="Permalink to “ADR-0161: Ports are types; implementations are named for what they do”">​</a></h1><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to “Context”">​</a></h2><p>ADR-0160 fixed the packaging (a <code>lib-*</code> repo is a Handl package, not a crate) and established that a repo whose <em>implementation</em> is Rust keeps its crate. Working through the 18 remaining hybrids showed that &quot;keeps a crate&quot; is not enough: it does not say <strong>what</strong> the repo is. Today a repo like <code>lib-base64</code> mixes three things — a package source, a metadata table, and (in some cases) real Rust — and a port like <code>lib-encoding</code> mixes an interface with a dispatch helper.</p><p>Two rules were missing.</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision”">​</a></h2><p><strong>1. A port is types and laws only.</strong> <code>lib-&lt;port&gt;</code> declares the shape (<code>op</code>/signature + law today; the categorical form replaces that syntax later) and carries <strong>no Rust</strong>. A one-line pure-Handl dispatch that belongs to the <em>shape</em> may live there (e.g. <code>encoding.encode := (v, c) ~&gt; (c.encode)(v)</code>).</p><p><strong>2. An implementation is a separate repo, named for its language or target.</strong><code>lib-&lt;port&gt;-handl</code> (pure Handl), <code>lib-&lt;port&gt;-c</code> (C FFI), <code>lib-&lt;port&gt;-native</code> (Rust std). Only these may carry a <code>Cargo.toml</code>.</p><p><strong>3. Composition is by <strong>imported value</strong>, never by an aggregate.</strong> <code>encode(v, b64)</code> where <code>b64</code> comes from <code>from &quot;base64&quot; import codec</code>. No <code>formats = { base64, hex }</code> registry: an aggregate would pull every codec into the graph and defeat tree-shaking.</p><p><strong>4. A shared helper is named for the capability it provides, not for the port it serves.</strong> If <code>json-handl</code> and <code>toml-handl</code> both need number/string/list scanning, the extraction is <code>lib-literal-handl</code> — not <code>lib-parse-handl</code>, which would imply a default implementation of the <code>parse</code> port that does not exist.</p><p><strong>5. The loader resolves lazily, per module.</strong> Tree-shaking is a loader property: <code>module_in_dir(pkg, module)</code> + <code>PackageResolver::lazy</code> read exactly one file, so importing <code>base64</code> never touches <code>hex</code>. <code>pkg.hnl</code> <code>deps</code> therefore describe module dependencies, not package ones.</p><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequences”">​</a></h2><ul><li>Applied to the current backlog: <code>lib-parse</code>, <code>lib-type</code>, <code>lib-encoding</code> become ports; <code>lib-json-handl</code>, <code>lib-base64-handl</code>, <code>lib-hex-handl</code> become impls; <code>lib-base64</code>, <code>lib-hex</code>, <code>lib-json</code> stop being repos of their own.</li><li>Class C (the 10 Rust-only backends) resolves the same way: <code>lib-cmp</code> keeps the axioms, <code>lib-cmp-handl</code> or <code>lib-cmp-native</code> carries the code.</li><li>Ports never appear in <code>clone-gate</code> failures for missing Rust; impls never appear in package manifests as crates.</li><li><strong>Cost:</strong> more repos than &quot;one repo per port&quot; — the price of both tree-shaking and a library that can gain a second backend without touching the contract.</li></ul><h2 id="alternatives-rejected" tabindex="-1">Alternatives rejected <a class="header-anchor" href="#alternatives-rejected" aria-label="Permalink to “Alternatives rejected”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>One repo per port holding its impl</strong> — the current state; forbids a second backend and forces the whole port into every consumer.</li><li><strong>Aggregate registry (<code>formats = {…}</code>)</strong> — simple to write, defeats tree-shaking; verified as a requirement by the &quot;import base64, never touch hex&quot; test in <code>handl_plugin_lib_handl::pkg</code>.</li><li><strong>Naming impls after the port (<code>lib-parse-handl</code>)</strong> — implies a default parser that the port&#39;s own signature (<code>parse(input, parser)</code>) shows does not exist.</li></ul>`,14)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};